course: space
lesson: 11
title: Jupiter's moons
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jupiter is like a king with a huge court: more than 90 moons go round it, and telescopes keep finding new tiny ones.
explain: Four of them are big worlds of their own, first seen by Galileo in 1610: Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto.
explain: Each is different: Io is covered in erupting volcanoes, Europa hides an ocean under its ice, Ganymede is the biggest moon of all, and Callisto is battered with craters.
explain: Think of a playground roundabout with three children holding on at different distances: Io, Europa and Ganymede go round in a steady beat, Io four times while Europa goes twice and Ganymede once.
explain: Their eclipses, as they slip into Jupiter's shadow, happen so regularly that in the 1600s people used them as a clock in the sky to find their longitude.
fact: Jupiter has more than 90 known moons, and the count is still rising as telescopes find more tiny ones.
fact: Galileo discovered Jupiter's four biggest moons, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, in 1610 with one of the first telescopes.
fact: Io is the most volcanically active world in the Solar System, with hundreds of volcanoes spraying sulphur plumes.
fact: Io's yellow, orange and red patches come from sulphur and sulphur compounds spread by its volcanoes.
fact: Europa has an icy crust over a salty ocean of liquid water that may hold about twice as much water as all the Earth's oceans.
fact: NASA's Europa Clipper launched in 2024 and should reach Jupiter in 2030 to study whether Europa could support life.
fact: Ganymede is the largest moon in the Solar System, wider than the planet Mercury.
fact: Ganymede is the only moon known to have its own magnetic field.
fact: Callisto is the most heavily cratered object in the Solar System, with a very old surface hardly changed for about 4 billion years.
fact: For every one orbit of Ganymede, Europa goes round twice and Io four times.
fact: Europe's JUICE spacecraft, launched in 2023, will reach Jupiter in 2031 and end by orbiting Ganymede.
fact: In the 1600s the regular eclipses of Jupiter's moons were used as a clock in the sky to work out longitude, how far east or west you are, on land.
exercise: How many known moons does Jupiter have? | More than 90, and the count is still rising | Exactly four, the ones Galileo saw | Just one, like the Earth | None, giant planets cannot hold moons
exercise: Who discovered Jupiter's four biggest moons, and when? | Galileo, in 1610 | Isaac Newton, in 1687 | William Herschel, in 1781 | Ptolemy, about 150 AD
exercise: What did Galileo use to find Jupiter's big moons? | One of the first telescopes | A space probe sent to Jupiter | Just his own naked eyes and patience | A giant radio dish on a hill
exercise: Which of Jupiter's moons is the most volcanically active world in the Solar System? | Io | Europa | Callisto | Ganymede
exercise: Why is Io yellow, orange and red? | Sulphur spread by its volcanoes | Rust like the dust on Mars | Light reflected from the Sun's flames | Forests of strange red plants
exercise: What is under Europa's icy crust? | A salty ocean of liquid water | Solid rock right to the middle | A sea of hot lava | Empty caves full of air
exercise: About how much water may Europa's ocean hold compared with all the Earth's oceans? | About twice as much | About a hundredth as much | Exactly the same amount | About a thousand times more
exercise: What is NASA's Europa Clipper going to study? | Whether Europa could support life | Whether Jupiter has a solid surface | How fast Io's volcanoes cool | Where Jupiter's rings came from
exercise: Which is the largest moon in the Solar System? | Ganymede | Our Moon | Io | Titan, Saturn's moon
exercise: Ganymede is wider than which planet? | Mercury | The Earth | Mars | Venus
exercise: What is special about Ganymede apart from its size? | It is the only moon known to have its own magnetic field | It is the only moon with a thick atmosphere | It is the only moon with volcanoes | It is the only moon that orbits backwards
exercise: Which object is the most heavily cratered in the Solar System? | Callisto | Io | Europa | The Earth
exercise: Why does Callisto keep so many craters? | Its surface has hardly changed for about 4 billion years | Its volcanoes keep making new craters | It is hit far more often than other moons | Its ice melts and refreezes into round holes
exercise: While Ganymede goes round Jupiter once, how many times does Io go round? | Four times | Twice | Once | Eight times
exercise: Which spacecraft will end its mission by orbiting Ganymede? | Europe's JUICE | NASA's Europa Clipper | Voyager 2 | Cassini
exercise: When should JUICE reach Jupiter? | In 2031 | In 2024 | In 2050 | It got there in 1610
exercise: What were the eclipses of Jupiter's moons used for in the 1600s? | A clock in the sky to work out longitude | Forecasting the weather a whole week ahead | Choosing the right date of Easter each spring | Measuring the height of mountains far away
test: My mate says the Earth's Moon is the biggest moon there is. right? | No, Ganymede is the biggest, wider than Mercury | Yes, our Moon is the biggest of all, it just looks small | No, Io is the biggest, because it is hottest | Yes, moons can never be bigger than our Moon
test: In a cartoon, Io is drawn as a grey, dead ball like our Moon. What should it look like? | Yellow, orange and red, with erupting volcanoes | Pure white, covered in smooth ice | Blue, covered all over by a deep open ocean of water | Black, with no light at all
test: why would anyone send a probe all the way to Europa, it's just ice innit | Its ice may hide a huge ocean that could support life | It is made of gold under the ice | It is the closest moon to the Earth | It has the most volcanoes in the Solar System
test: If Europa goes round Jupiter 10 times, about how many times does Io go round in the same time? | 20 | 10 | 5 | 40
test: A sailor in 1680 has an accurate table of when Jupiter's moons slip into its shadow. What can he work out from it on land? | His longitude | The depth of the sea | Tomorrow's weather | His age
test: Which is true? | Jupiter's moon count keeps rising as telescopes find tiny new moons | Jupiter has exactly four moons and always will | Scientists stopped looking for moons in 1610 | Jupiter loses a moon every year
test: Sam reckons Callisto must be young cos it's covered in craters. what's wrong with that? | Many craters mean an old surface that has hardly changed | Craters only form on young moons | Callisto has no craters at all | Craters are made by Callisto's volcanoes
test: Two spacecraft are on their way to Jupiter's moons. Which pair is right? | Europa Clipper for Europa, JUICE ending at Ganymede | Voyager for Io, and the old Apollo craft for Callisto | JUICE for Io, Clipper for Callisto | Cassini for Europa, Huygens for Ganymede
test: lol my sister thinks Io's yellow cos it's made of cheese. what really makes it yellow | Sulphur from its volcanoes | Yellow sand blown from Jupiter | Sunlight bouncing off ice | Gold dust from space
test: A game shows a moon with its own magnetic field going round Jupiter. Which moon can it be? | Ganymede | Callisto | Io | Europa
test: Why could Galileo see only four of Jupiter's moons? | The others are tiny and far too faint for his telescope | Jupiter had only four moons in 1610 | The others hide behind Jupiter all the time | The other moons appeared after his death
exam: State the number of natural satellites presently known to orbit the planet Jupiter. | In excess of ninety | Precisely four, as Galileo saw | Precisely one, as the Earth has | None whatsoever, at present
exam: Identify the satellite of Jupiter which is distinguished as the largest moon within the Solar System. | Ganymede | Io | Europa | Callisto
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the interior of Europa. | A saline ocean of liquid water lies beneath its icy crust | Its interior consists entirely of molten iron | It is hollow and filled with gas | It is composed throughout of solid sulphur
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which the eclipses of the satellites of Jupiter were employed during the seventeenth century. | The determination of longitude on land | The prediction of tides | The measurement of the Earth's mass | The calculation of the date of Easter each year
